<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">324 BCE · Opis, on the Tigris</p><h1 class="sp-title">Alexander's Speech at Opis</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>At Opis on the Tigris, Alexander announced that he would send home his worn-out Macedonian veterans, and his army, already angry at his growing reliance on Persian troops and customs, mutinied. According to Arrian, Alexander answered with a furious speech reminding them what his father and he had made of them.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The words</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Philip found you vagabonds and destitute of means, most of you clad in hides, feeding a few sheep up the mountain sides.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Arrian, Anabasis of Alexander, VII.9, tr. E. J. Chinnock (1884).</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>After three days of standoff the Macedonians begged forgiveness, and Alexander held a feast of reconciliation at which Macedonians and Persians drank together and prayed for concord between the peoples. He died at Babylon the following year, and his empire was divided among his generals.</p></section>
